hile keeping all honor tiles intact for pursuing high-value patterns like Little Three Dragons (64 points).\n\nEvaluation: Discarding W6 allows focusing on more promising sequences in other suits and maintaining honor tiles for dragon-based patterns. Other discards like winds or key sequence tiles might reduce opportunities for high-scoring hands.\n\nSo Answer should be Play W6.", "output": "Evaluation: Your hand contains a mix of suited and honor tiles with no exposed melds. Key observations:  \n- You hold J1 and J3 (dragons), making Little Three Dragons (64 points) a highly viable target if you acquire J2 and additional dragon tiles. This pattern is prioritized over lower-value options like Big Three Winds (12 points) due to its significant point contribution.  \n- Suited tiles show potential for sequences: T2/T4 could form a 2-3-4 chow with T3; B2/B3 could form 1-2-3 or 2-3-4 with B1/B4; B6/B8 could form 6-7-8 with B7. However, W6 is isolated\u2014no adjacent Character tiles (W1/W9 are too distant)\u2014and offers minimal sequence flexibility compared to other suited tiles.  \n- Winds (F2/F3/F4) are less critical; discarding one wouldn't hinder Little Three Dragons, but keeping all honors supports fallback patterns like All Honors (64 points) if dragon acquisition stalls.  \n- Discarding W6 preserves terminals (W1/W9) for outside-hand patterns and avoids weakening existing sequence potentials (e.g., discarding B6 would disrupt B6/B8 synergy for 6-7-8).  \n\nReason: W6 is the least versatile tile\u2014it cannot contribute to immediate chow formations and blocks efficient hand development toward high-value patterns. Retaining J1/J3 is essential for Little Three Dragons, and discarding W6 maintains stronger sequence options in Bamboo/Dots while keeping all honors intact. Other discards (e.g., winds) sacrifice flexibility without significant gain, as wind-based patterns yield fewer points.  \n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
