le like 8 might make others think you don't have 7 or 9, but not always.\n\nBut for simplicity, since no information, I should just discard based on my hand.\n\nAnother idea: I have F2,F3,F4, all winds. If I discard one wind, say F3, it might be safe because winds are less commonly used in sequences, but I have three different, so discarding one might free up for getting duplicates.\n\nFor example, if I discard F3, then I have F2 and F4, so if I draw another F2, I have pung of South.\n\nBut South is not prevalent or seat wind, so Dragon Pung would apply, but for winds, it's \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" which is 1 point, or \"Prevalent Wind\" etc, but only if it's East.\n\nSince prevalent and seat are East, F1 is valuable.\n\nF2,F3,F4 are less valuable.\n\nSo having pung of South gives only 1 point for \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" since winds are honors.\n\nSimilarly for dragons.\n\nBut 1 point is minimal.\n\nWhereas for knitted, I can get 12 points.\n\nSo better to pursue knitted.\n\nMoreover, with honors, I can have multiple small fans.\n\nBut 12 points is substantial.\n\nNow, back to discarding.\n\nLet's see if any of the non-pool tiles can be part of other patterns.\n\nFor example, W1, W6, W9: if I had W2,W3 etc, but not, so hard to form character suit hand.\n\nSimilarly, dots: B2,B3,B6,B8 \u2014 with B2,B3, I can aim for 1,2,3 if I get B1, or 2,3,4 with B4.\n\nB6,B8, need B7 for 6,7,8 or B5,B7 for 5,6,7 etc.\n\nBut B1 is terminal, might be scarce.\n\nT2,T4, need T3 for 2,3,4.\n\nT3 is available.\n\nBut for knitted, T3 is not in pool, since Group B needs T2,T5,T8, not T3.\n\nT3 is irrelevant for knitted straight.\n\nSimilarly, B1 not relevant.\n\nSo probably best to discard non-pool.\n\nNow, among W6, W9, T4, B2, B8, which one to discard.\n\nI am considering discarding T4.\n\nWhy? Because T4 is Bamboo 4, and for the knitted straight, if Bamboo is Group B, it needs 2,5,8, so 4 is not needed. Moreover, 4 is not a terminal, so less critical for other patterns.